---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
import WangcaiSeries from '../../components/WangcaiSeries.astro';
const P = '/misc/tahoe-2026/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Two Capitols and a Very Clear Lake — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Five days at the end of summer 2026: Sacramento, a pedal boat on Lake Tahoe, Carson City and Reno, Emerald Bay and Fannette Island, then Santa Cruz and Carmel, with our dog Wangcai.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · August 2026</p>
  <h1>Two Capitols and a Very Clear Lake</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Five slow days at the end of summer: Sacramento, Lake Tahoe, a corner of Nevada and the coast, with Wangcai riding along.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>5 days · August 22–26, 2026</strong><br />
    Sacramento → South Lake Tahoe → Stateline → Carson City → Reno → Tahoe City → Sugar Pine Point → Emerald Bay and
    Fannette Island → Santa Cruz → Carmel-by-the-Sea<br />
    Two states, California and Nevada · two state capitols in two days · roughly 530 miles between the first photo
    and the last · one red pedal boat · one island with a ruined tea house on top · one cream-colored dog in a gingham bandana
  </div>

  <p>
    This one was meant to be easy. My summer internship was ending, and before the next thing began we wanted a few
    days that asked nothing of anybody. So we put Wangcai in the back seat and drove a loose loop through Northern
    California and a corner of Nevada. It started in Sacramento and climbed to Lake Tahoe. It dipped into Carson City
    and Reno, came back to the lake's West Shore, and then ran all the way down to the Pacific at Santa Cruz and
    Carmel. Nobody was keeping score, and most of the days in the camera roll don't start until lunchtime.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.webp'} alt="Map of the whole trip: Sacramento east to South Lake Tahoe, north to Carson City and Reno, back to Lake Tahoe's West Shore and Emerald Bay, then southwest to Santa Cruz and Carmel." /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole loop, in the order the photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    As with the other trips on this page, everything here is reconstructed from the camera roll. A phone keeps a time
    and a place for every picture, and lined up in order they make an honest, if patchy, diary. Where the photos are
    silent, I've said so rather than guess. Where a place has a story worth knowing, I've added a little of it.
    And as with <a href="/misc/spring-break-2026">the spring trip</a>, the real protagonist is
    <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">Wangcai</a>, who came home with us in January 2025 and has been in the back seat of
    every road trip since. She wore a red gingham bandana for much of this one.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Sacramento</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0822.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0822.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1 in Sacramento, from the State Capitol across the river to the Ziggurat and back to Old Sacramento, then the evening drive up to South Lake Tahoe." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: the Capitol, a loop across the river, an afternoon on the Old Sacramento waterfront, then up to the lake.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The Capitol and its mall</h3>
  <p>
    Sacramento is the kind of state capital people drive past on I-80 on the way to somewhere else. That's a pity,
    because its center is handsome and very green. We had arrived in Sacramento late on the Friday night and stayed at
    The Citizen Hotel, so the trip really began with waking up downtown. We started at noon at the <strong>California State
    Capitol</strong>, a white neoclassical building finished in 1874. Its dome is a cousin of the one in Washington,
    lifted on a ring of columns. Around it lies Capitol Park, a forty-acre arboretum of trees planted from all over the
    world. That Saturday the sky was a flat, cloudless blue, the kind you only get in the Central Valley in late
    summer, and the dome stood out against it like a wedding cake.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sac-capitol.webp'} alt="The white California State Capitol with its dome, framed by tall pines and palms under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The California State Capitol from 10th Street.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sac-capitol-steps.webp'} alt="The front of the California State Capitol with its columned portico and dome, a few visitors on the path." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Up close: the portico and the dome.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Turn around on the Capitol's west steps and you are looking straight down <strong>Capitol Mall</strong>, a wide
    boulevard that runs about a mile, past office towers and lawns, to the river. At the far end, small and gold
    in the haze, is the Tower Bridge. The axis is deliberate: the seat of government at one end, the Sacramento River
    at the other. In the Gold Rush that river was the reason the city existed at all, the place where miners stepped
    off the boats from San Francisco and headed for the hills.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capitol-mall.webp'} alt="Capitol Mall stretching away from the Capitol toward downtown towers, with the gold Tower Bridge just visible at the end." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Capitol Mall, with the Tower Bridge at the far end.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Across the street to the west stands a building that looks as if it should be in Washington, D.C.: the
    <strong>Stanley Mosk Library and Courts Building</strong>, finished in 1928, with a row of tall columns, a
    sculpted pediment and a fountain splashing in a round pool out front. It is home to the California State Library,
    and it faces the Capitol across the park like a twin built in a different mood. Where the Capitol is all
    nineteenth-century wedding cake, this is twentieth-century Beaux-Arts, severe and grand.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanley-mosk.webp'} alt="The neoclassical Stanley Mosk Library and Courts Building with tall columns and a sculpted pediment, a fountain in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Stanley Mosk Library and Courts Building.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Across the river and back</h3>
  <p>
    After an hour we got back in the car and drove across the river into West Sacramento, past one building in particular. <strong>The Ziggurat</strong> is exactly what it sounds like: an eleven-story stepped pyramid in
    pale stone, built in the late 1990s for a lending company and later taken over by the state as offices. It sits on
    the riverbank like something from a lost civilization that took up accounting. We photographed it through the
    windshield and kept going; the circle back over the I Street Bridge took a few minutes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ziggurat.webp'} alt="The Ziggurat, a beige stepped-pyramid office building in West Sacramento, seen through a car windshield." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Ziggurat, through the windshield.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Back on the Sacramento side, we parked and walked the riverfront in Old Sacramento. A long ramp leads down to the
    docks, and from it you get the Ziggurat again, now across the water, next to a curved blue glass tower. Wangcai led
    the way down the ramp on her leash, a cream-colored blur against the gray metal grating.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-riverwalk.webp'} alt="Wangcai, a cream-colored dog on a leash, walking toward the camera on a riverfront ramp, with the Ziggurat and a glass tower across the river." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the Old Sacramento riverwalk, the Ziggurat behind her.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Crab by the Tower Bridge</h3>
  <p>
    Lunch was late, at two, on the river: a crab restaurant built out over the water on stilts, with the
    <strong>Tower Bridge</strong> filling the view. The bridge is a vertical-lift bridge from 1935. Its whole middle
    span can rise between the two towers to let tall boats through. For decades it was painted a dull silver-green,
    then in 2002 it was repainted gold, which is the only correct color for a bridge in a Gold Rush town. From the
    restaurant deck, under a yellow umbrella, it looked close enough to touch.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'old-sac-waterfront.webp'} alt="A restaurant on stilts over the Sacramento River, with docks below and the gold Tower Bridge behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The waterfront, with the Tower Bridge behind.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tower-bridge.webp'} alt="The gold Tower Bridge, a vertical-lift bridge with two tall towers, over the green Sacramento River under wispy clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Tower Bridge (1935), gold since 2002.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    What arrived was a seafood boil in a metal pan: crab legs swimming in a deep orange sauce of butter and spice,
    with bread for dipping. The camera roll has more than a dozen pictures of this pan, taken over the space of a
    minute. They record, in sequence, a piece of bread going into the sauce and coming out considerably heavier.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'crab-boil.webp'} alt="Close-up of crab legs in an orange buttery spiced sauce in a metal pan." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The crab boil. The bread did not survive.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Delta King and the old bridges</h3>
  <p>
    Afterwards we went aboard the <strong>Delta King</strong>, a big white sternwheeler permanently
    moored at Old Sacramento. She was built in 1927 and, with her twin the Delta Queen, ran overnight between
    Sacramento and San Francisco, a ten-hour trip down the river and across the bay. The overnight boats lost out to
    highways in the 1940s, and the King had a long, rough retirement, including years half-sunk, before she was
    restored and brought back here. Her paddlewheel is still on the stern, and the gangway still says "Welcome Aboard".
    Inside, the dining room was laid with white tablecloths, the bar looks out on the I Street Bridge, and a
    framed photograph shows her in 1984, listing in San Francisco Bay after fifteen months partly under water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'delta-king.webp'} alt="The white sternwheeler Delta King moored at Old Sacramento, with a Delta King sign over the gangway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Delta King, built 1927.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'delta-king-1984.webp'} alt="An old photograph of the Delta King listing badly, her lower decks under water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same boat in 1984, from a display on board.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Old Sacramento itself is a few blocks of Gold Rush–era brick and wooden boardwalks. It was the western end of the
    Pony Express and the starting point of the Central Pacific, the western half of the first transcontinental
    railroad. From the end of the docks, looking upriver, you can see the <strong>I Street Bridge</strong>, an old steel
    truss from 1911 that swings sideways on a pivot to let boats pass, with trains on one deck and cars on the other.
    It was my last photo of the day, at 3:42 in the afternoon.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'old-sac-docks.webp'} alt="Wooden docks and pilings along the Old Sacramento waterfront, with a small riverboat and the Tower Bridge in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Old Sacramento docks.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i-street-bridge.webp'} alt="The steel truss I Street Bridge spanning the wide Sacramento River, with mooring pilings in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The I Street Bridge (1911), a swing bridge.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Before we left Old Sacramento there was ice cream from an old-fashioned sweets shop. Then we drove up to South
    Lake Tahoe, to the Hampton Inn &amp; Suites for the night, and by dark we were on the sand at the Ski Run beach
    with a takeout dinner.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-ski-run-night.webp'} alt="Wangcai in her red gingham bandana sitting on the sand at night, a lit pier pavilion and a moored boat behind her." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the beach at Ski Run, after dark.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: Lake Tahoe, then Nevada</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0823.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0823.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2, from South Lake Tahoe to Stateline, Carson City and Reno, then back over to Truckee for the night." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: the lake, then down the other side to two Nevada cities.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>South Lake Tahoe</h3>
  <p>
    The drive from Sacramento to Lake Tahoe is one long climb. You leave the valley floor near sea level, wind up
    through the foothills of Gold Country and the pine forests of the Sierra Nevada, cross a pass above seven thousand
    feet, and drop down to the lake at about 6,200. We had made that drive the evening before, and by lunchtime on
    Sunday we were at the marina at the bottom of
    Ski Run Boulevard in <strong>South Lake Tahoe</strong>: a sandy beach, rows of rental boats, a lawn with a path
    along the water, and beyond all of it the lake, with the mountains of the far shore faint and blue.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ski-run-marina.webp'} alt="A grassy lakeside path at South Lake Tahoe beside a marina full of boats, the lake and distant mountains under streaky clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The marina at Ski Run, South Lake Tahoe.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Lake Tahoe is hard to describe by numbers, though the numbers are remarkable. It is twenty-two miles long and
    sixteen across. It sits on the border of California and Nevada, and it is more than 1,600 feet deep, the second
    deepest lake in the country after Crater Lake. It holds so much water that, as the local saying goes, if you
    spread it over California it would cover the state more than a foot deep. Mostly, though, it is blue, a blue that
    gets darker as the bottom drops away until, a few hundred yards out, it is nearly navy.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before the boat we looked into a souvenir shop, where a big brown bear stood upright in the aisle. Wangcai sat
    down in front of it for her photo.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-bear-shop.webp'} alt="Wangcai in her bandana sitting on a wooden floor in a souvenir shop aisle, in front of a large standing brown bear figure." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The first bear of the trip.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Wangcai takes the helm</h3>
  <p>
    So we rented a red pedal boat and took Wangcai out on it. This, it turns out, was the right decision. She sat up
    front in her bandana with her ears straight up, facing into the wind like the figurehead on a ship. In every photo from the boat her
    ears are up, and her nose is pointed at the open water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-pedal-boat.webp'} alt="Wangcai in a red gingham bandana on the bow of a red pedal boat, ears up, looking out over the lake under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai, on lookout.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-tour-boat.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a red pedal boat looking out at a large white tour boat on Lake Tahoe." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Watching a much bigger boat go by.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A big white tour boat passed while we were out, a floating hotel compared to our plastic tub, and Wangcai turned
    her head to watch it. Then she looked straight into the phone, her face a little damp from the spray, with an
    expression somewhere between patient and unimpressed.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-boat-closeup.webp'} alt="Close-up of Wangcai's face on the pedal boat, slightly wet, looking at the camera with the lake behind her." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Slightly splashed, not amused.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From the water, the shore looks the way Tahoe is supposed to look: a strip of beach, a line of pines, and a mountain
    rising straight up behind them, with clouds stacked over the top. Near the shore the water was a startling bottle
    green, and you could see the sandy bottom through it even from a pedal boat. That clarity is Tahoe's real fame.
    The water is so clear that scientists have measured it for more than half a century by lowering a white disk on a
    rope and noting the depth at which it disappears.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lake-from-boat.webp'} alt="Green-blue lake water from a pedal boat, with a forested shore and a mountain under puffy white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lake Tahoe from the pedal boat.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Stateline</h3>
  <p>
    Before crossing into Nevada we stopped for bubble tea, a purple layered drink. A mile up the road, South Lake
    Tahoe ends and Nevada begins, and you can tell exactly where. On the California side
    are motels, ski shops and pine trees. Cross the line and suddenly there are high-rise casinos, because gambling is
    legal on one side and not the other, so the towers crowd right up against the border. The town on the Nevada side
    is called, with admirable directness, <strong>Stateline</strong>. We walked the strip for half an hour in the
    afternoon sun, past glass towers that reflected the sky and the mountains back at themselves. From high up in one of the
    towers, South Lake Tahoe turns out to be mostly forest, with the lake beyond it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stateline-tower.webp'} alt="A tall blue glass casino tower at Stateline, Nevada, reflecting clouds and pines, with a Nevada flag below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A casino tower at Stateline, the Nevada flag out front.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stateline-view.webp'} alt="South Lake Tahoe seen from high up: a carpet of pine forest running to the lake shore, with mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>South Lake Tahoe from above: mostly pines.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Carson City</h3>
  <p>
    From Stateline the road climbs over the ridge on the east side of the lake and then drops, steeply, into the
    Carson Valley, out of the pines and into sagebrush. Half an hour later we were in <strong>Carson City</strong>,
    Nevada's capital. It is named, indirectly, after Kit Carson,
    the frontier scout. The <strong>Nevada State Capitol</strong> is a modest, lovely building from 1871, built of
    sandstone quarried by inmates at the nearby state prison. On top sits a silver-painted cupola, a nod to the
    Silver State and the Comstock Lode silver that paid for much of early Nevada.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nevada-capitol.webp'} alt="The Nevada State Capitol, a sandstone building with white trim and silver cupolas, seen from the side." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Nevada State Capitol (1871), from the side.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nevada-capitol-front.webp'} alt="The front of the Nevada State Capitol with its white portico and silver cupola, framed by shade trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The front, through the trees.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After Sacramento's grandeur, this was a different idea of what a capitol should be. It feels more like a county
    courthouse in a small town, set in a shady park with old trees and a scattering of plaques. Nevada became a state
    in 1864, in the middle of the Civil War, which is why "Battle Born" is on its flag. The capitol was built just a
    few years later, when the whole state had only a few tens of thousands of people.
  </p>
  <p>
    The newer state buildings cluster around the old one. Just south stands the <strong>Supreme Court of
    Nevada</strong>, a cream-colored building with a columned portico of its own. On a wall nearby, the Great Seal of
    the State of Nevada is set into the stone in colored terrazzo. It shows mountains, a rising sun, a quartz mill, a
    train crossing a trestle and a farmer's wheat, which together sum up how nineteenth-century Nevada made its
    living. Around the rim runs the state motto, "All for Our Country".
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nevada-supreme-court.webp'} alt="The cream-colored Supreme Court of Nevada building with a columned portico and a lawn in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Supreme Court of Nevada.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nevada-seal.webp'} alt="The Great Seal of the State of Nevada inlaid in a stone wall, with mountains, a sun, a mill and a train." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Great Seal: "All for Our Country".</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Reno and the Truckee River</h3>
  <p>
    By a quarter to seven we were thirty miles north, in <strong>Reno</strong>, walking along the Truckee River
    downtown. The Truckee is Lake Tahoe's only outlet. It leaves the lake at Tahoe City, tumbles down through the
    canyon, runs straight through the middle of Reno and ends in Pyramid Lake out in the desert. So the water rushing
    past us had been in Lake Tahoe not long before, which felt like a neat way to tie the day together. Wangcai
    took a different view of the river. It was full of ducks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-truckee.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a leash at the top of stone steps by the Truckee River in Reno, watching a flock of ducks on the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai watches the ducks on the Truckee.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    This stretch of river has been rebuilt as a whitewater park. Boulders have been placed to make small rapids and
    pools, and there are steps down to the water and footbridges across it. On an August evening it is full of people
    wading and dogs swimming. The light was going gold, and across the river a stone church spire stood against the
    sky next to a glassy modern office block. Reno is like that: a casino town with a very pleasant river running
    through it that most visitors never see.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'truckee-footbridge.webp'} alt="A dark steel footbridge hung with flower baskets crossing the Truckee River's small rapids in Reno, under shade trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A footbridge over the whitewater park.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'truckee-church.webp'} alt="The Truckee River in evening light with rocks and reeds, a church spire and an office building on the far bank." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Truckee River in downtown Reno, around 6:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That is where the photos for Sunday stop. For the night we crossed back into California, to a lodge in the Martis Valley outside Truckee.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 3: The West Shore and Emerald Bay</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0824.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0824.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3 on Lake Tahoe's West Shore, from Sugar Pine Point south to the Emerald Bay overlook, Vikingsholm and Fannette Island." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: Sugar Pine Point, then Emerald Bay from above, from the shore, and from the island.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Tahoe City</h3>
  <p>
    Monday was the day for the lake itself. Coming down from Truckee, we stopped first in <strong>Tahoe City</strong>,
    at the lake's north-west corner, for an açaí bowl and a look along the waterfront. This is where the Truckee
    River leaves the lake, the same river we had watched in Reno the evening before.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tahoe-city.webp'} alt="The Tahoe City waterfront: a promenade edge and a dock, boats on blue water, forested shore and puffy clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The waterfront at Tahoe City, a little after noon.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Sugar Pine Point</h3>
  <p>
    By early afternoon we were at <strong>Sugar Pine Point State
    Park</strong> on Tahoe's West Shore, a big stretch of pine forest that runs right down to the water. In 1960 it
    hosted the cross-country skiing events of the Winter Olympics at nearby Squaw Valley, now Palisades Tahoe. In summer it is quiet:
    a nature center, picnic tables under enormous trees, and an old lakeside estate from the turn of the century.
    By the nature center stood a painted board of Tahoe wildlife with holes cut out for faces: a chipmunk, an eagle
    and a black bear.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-bear.webp'} alt="A painted wildlife board with a chipmunk, an eagle and a bear, with Wangcai's white face poking through the bear's face hole." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A new species of Tahoe black bear.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The camera roll contains thirteen photographs of this board, taken in under a minute, and the bear's face changes
    from one to the next. In several of them it is Wangcai's: white, ears tucked behind the edges of the hole,
    tongue out. It is clearly the best bear in the park.
  </p>
  <p>
    From there a lakefront trail runs along the shore under tall pines. It isn't a hike so much as a stroll on a
    paved path, with benches and picnic tables and glimpses of blue between the trunks. Wangcai led,
    leaning into her leash and stopping every few yards to smell something important. The pines here are huge,
    with thick, cracked orange bark, and the light under them comes down in patches.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-lakefront-trail.webp'} alt="Wangcai trotting along a shaded paved path under tall pines, with picnic tables and blue lake water beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the lakefront trail at Sugar Pine Point.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The trail comes out above a long wooden pier with a little brown boathouse halfway along it. The water around the
    pier is the color people mean when they say "Tahoe blue". Near the shore it is pale turquoise over white sand,
    then it turns jade over the shallows, then it drops off into deep blue. Wangcai sat on the path for her portrait
    with the pier behind her, tongue out.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sugar-pine-pines.webp'} alt="The Sugar Pine Point pier seen between two tall pines, over turquoise water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pier between the pines.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-pier.webp'} alt="Wangcai sitting on a path, mouth open, with a wooden pier and brown boathouse in turquoise water behind her." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai and the Sugar Pine Point pier.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It's worth looking at the water itself for a minute, because it doesn't look like a lake. Looking down from the
    shore, you can see every rock on the bottom several feet down, as clear as through glass. The shadow of a
    cloud passing overhead made a dark patch on the lake floor, sharp-edged, as if someone had spilled ink in a
    swimming pool. The far shore, ten miles away in Nevada, was a crisp line of blue mountains with white cumulus
    piling up above them.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sugar-pine-pier.webp'} alt="A long wooden pier with a brown boathouse over clear turquoise water, a dark cloud shadow on the lake bottom." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pier and boathouse, water clear to the bottom.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sugar-pine-clear-water.webp'} alt="Very clear shallow lake water over a rocky bottom, shading from turquoise to deep blue, with mountains on the far shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tahoe blue, in three shades.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Wangcai found her own way down to the water. She picked her way through the bushes onto the granite boulders at
    the edge and put her nose right down to the lake, as if she were checking its temperature. A few minutes later,
    she was wading along the shoreline in the small waves, soaked to the shoulders.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-shore.webp'} alt="Wangcai among green shrubs on granite boulders, nose down toward the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Testing the water.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-wading.webp'} alt="Wangcai wading in the small waves along the rocky shore of Lake Tahoe." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wading in.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Emerald Bay from above</h3>
  <p>
    Fifteen minutes south, the road along the West Shore climbs high above the lake and threads along a narrow ridge
    with drops on both sides. Below it lies <strong>Emerald Bay</strong>, the most photographed place on Lake Tahoe.
    It is a long, narrow inlet carved by a glacier, almost sealed off from the main lake by a moraine at its mouth.
    In 1969 it was declared a National Natural Landmark. We stopped at the overlook a little after three and walked out onto the
    bare granite slabs above the bay, where a single bleached snag stands guard. This part of the day was a
    two-person affair: Wangcai stayed behind in the car, which keeps itself cool for her with the climate running
    (Tesla calls it Dog Mode), while we went down to the water.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'emerald-bay-overlook.webp'} alt="Granite slabs and a weathered pine at the Emerald Bay overlook, with the dark blue bay and Fannette Island below and white clouds above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Emerald Bay and Fannette Island from the overlook.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From up here the whole bay is laid out like a model. There are forested slopes on both sides, the narrow mouth
    to the right opening onto the big lake, and, in the middle, a tiny rocky island with a few pines on it. That is
    <strong>Fannette Island</strong>, the only island in all of Lake Tahoe. Hold that thought, because an hour later we
    would be standing on it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fannette-through-pines.webp'} alt="Fannette Island in the deep blue water of Emerald Bay, framed by the tops of pines, with a small boat and its wake." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fannette Island through the pines.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Down to Vikingsholm</h3>
  <p>
    From the road a steep trail drops about a mile down to the head of the bay, to <strong>Vikingsholm</strong>. It
    is a Scandinavian-style mansion built in 1929 for Lora Knight, a wealthy philanthropist who wanted a summer home
    that matched the fjord-like bay. The beach below it is a curve of white sand with a long wooden pier. When we got
    down there, a big white paddlewheeler, the <em>M.S. Dixie II</em>, was lying in the bay just off the pier. It is one of the tour boats that bring visitors across Lake Tahoe to see Emerald Bay from the water.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dixie-ii.webp'} alt="The white paddlewheel tour boat M.S. Dixie II beside a wooden pier in Emerald Bay, under towering white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The <em>M.S. Dixie II</em> off the Vikingsholm pier.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Down at water level the bay lives up to its name. Near shore the water was a clear, glassy green over pale
    rocks; behind it, the mountains at the head of the bay rose almost straight up, granite and pine, with one
    cloud parked on top. The GPS for this photo puts it out on the water, off the shore, and that is where we were:
    from the beach we set out for the island by kayak, a yellow two-person one.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'emerald-bay-water.webp'} alt="Clear green water of Emerald Bay at water level, with forested granite mountains rising behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Emerald Bay, at water level.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Fannette Island and the tea house</h3>
  <p>
    By 4:17 we had pulled the kayak up on the rocks and the photos were coming from the island itself. Fannette Island is a lump of granite about 150 feet high,
    with a few twisted junipers and pines clinging to it. Lora Knight, the same woman who built Vikingsholm, also
    built a tiny stone <strong>tea house</strong> on the summit of the island in 1929. The story goes that she
    had guests rowed out from the mansion for afternoon tea with the best view on the lake. The trail to the top
    winds up past an old juniper with a trunk as thick as a barrel.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fannette-juniper.jpg'} alt="A large, gnarled juniper tree on rocky ground on Fannette Island, with the bay and mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An old juniper on the way up.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fannette-tea-house.webp'} alt="The stone corner of the ruined tea house on Fannette Island, on granite above the deep blue bay." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tea house walls, at the summit.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Today the tea house is a roofless ruin, just four rough stone walls the size of a small room. The wooden window
    frames have survived, though, and they are the reason people make the trip. Each one frames a different piece of
    the bay: through one, the forested slopes and the far shore; through another, a rectangle of perfect deep blue
    water with a wall of pines above it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tea-house-window.webp'} alt="Emerald Bay and forested mountains seen through an empty wooden window frame in the stone walls of the tea house ruin." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from the tea house, framed.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tea-house-window-2.webp'} alt="Deep blue water and a wall of pines seen through another window frame in the tea house's granite walls." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Another window, another painting.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Through a third, the bay runs out between its forested points toward the open lake. It looks less like a view
    than a set of paintings someone hung on the walls, and it is very hard to stop taking photographs of it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tea-house-window-3.webp'} alt="The deep blue bay seen through a stone-walled window with a wooden frame, a white boat below and clouds over the far shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A third window, toward the mouth of the bay.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Step outside the walls and you look straight down the island's rocky flank into water that has turned a deep,
    saturated blue, almost purple in the middle of the bay where it is deepest. The far shore is a solid wall of
    pine. It was late afternoon, the sun was low enough to warm the granite, and apart from the occasional boat
    it was very quiet.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'emerald-bay-blue.jpg'} alt="Looking down from Fannette Island over pines and granite into the deep blue water of Emerald Bay, with a forested shore beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Emerald Bay from the top of Fannette Island.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>One last look from above</h3>
  <p>
    The trail back up from Vikingsholm is the price of the visit: a mile of trail that climbs some five hundred
    feet, all of it in the afternoon sun. We were back near the top at 5:29. From a spot beside the
    trail, looking down through the pines, the island we had just stood on looked tiny again, a toy island in
    a toy bay.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fannette-from-trail.webp'} alt="Emerald Bay and Fannette Island seen from high up through pines and shrubs in late-afternoon light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fannette Island from the trail, at 5:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Zoomed in, though, you could see everything we had just walked: the granite shoulders of the island, the
    junipers and pines on its ridge, and at the very top the small square stub of the tea house, gray against
    the trees. A single small motorboat was idling in its shadow. It was the last picture of the lake, and the
    best one.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fannette-island.webp'} alt="Fannette Island, a rocky pine-dotted island in deep blue Emerald Bay, seen from above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fannette Island, the only island in Lake Tahoe.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fannette-summit.webp'} alt="A close view of Fannette Island from above, with the small stone tea house ruin on its summit." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tea house on the summit, from across the bay.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Days 4 and 5: Santa Cruz and Carmel</h2>
  <p>
    The last two days traded the mountains for the sea.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0825.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0825.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 4 and 5, from Emerald Bay across California to Santa Cruz, then down the coast to Carmel-by-the-Sea." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 4–5: from the mountains to the sea, then a short hop down the coast.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The Chinatown gate</h3>
  <p>
    Between Emerald Bay and Santa Cruz lies the whole width of Northern California: down out of the Sierra, across the
    Central Valley, around the bottom of the Bay Area and over the Santa Cruz Mountains. The camera roll records none
    of it. We reached the Hotel Paradox in Santa Cruz close to midnight and stayed two nights. The next photo is from 11:31
    the following morning, on the riverwalk along the San Lorenzo River in
    <strong>Santa Cruz</strong>. It shows a yellow gate topped with two writhing dragons. The inscription across the
    top reads 纪念圣塔克鲁兹唐人街, "In memory of Santa Cruz Chinatown".
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chinatown-gate.webp'} alt="A yellow memorial archway topped with dragons on the Santa Cruz riverwalk, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Chinatown memorial gate on the San Lorenzo riverwalk.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is a memorial to a neighborhood that no longer exists. Santa Cruz had a series of Chinatowns from the 1860s on,
    home to Chinese workers who built the railroads and worked the farms and fisheries of the Monterey Bay. The last of
    them sat on low ground by the river and did not survive the great flood of 1955. Today it is parking lots and
    shops, and the gate is one of the few signs it was ever here.
  </p>

  <h3>The beach and the cliffs</h3>
  <p>
    After lunch with Wangcai on a café patio downtown, we went out to the <strong>Municipal Wharf</strong>, which runs nearly half a mile out into Monterey Bay
    and has done since 1914. From it you look along <strong>Main Beach</strong>: a wide, flat crescent of pale sand
    lined with palms, and behind it the Santa Cruz Beach Boardwalk. That's the seaside amusement park from 1907, the
    oldest surviving one in California, with its 1924 wooden roller coaster, the Giant Dipper. On a weekday at
    the end of August the beach was nearly empty.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'santa-cruz-main-beach.webp'} alt="Santa Cruz Main Beach, a wide sandy beach lined with palms, with the boardwalk and amusement park buildings beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Main Beach, with the Boardwalk beyond.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then we drove west along <strong>West Cliff Drive</strong>, which follows the top of the sea cliffs out of town,
    to its western end, at <strong>Natural Bridges State Beach</strong>. The name is
    now a little optimistic. There used to be three sandstone arches here; the outer ones collapsed into the sea over
    the twentieth century, and one is left, its top whitened by the seabirds that stand on it. The surf breaks white
    around it and runs up onto a small sandy cove.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'natural-bridges.webp'} alt="A sandstone sea arch with a white-topped rock off the cliffs at Natural Bridges, with waves and a sandy cove." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last natural bridge at Natural Bridges.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That was the end of the day's photos until night, when we found ourselves back at the riverwalk and the same
    dragon gate. Lit up from below, with a full-looking moon hanging exactly in the middle of the arch, it looked
    completely different, more like a stage set than a monument. Then back to the hotel for our second night in
    Santa Cruz.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chinatown-gate-night.webp'} alt="The Santa Cruz Chinatown memorial gate lit up at night, with the moon shining through the arch." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same gate at 8:39 pm, with the moon in the middle.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The last day</h3>
  <p>
    The last day was mostly road. Wangcai slept on the hotel bed that morning, then sat up on the front seat and
    looked out of the window. Around one we drove up through <strong>UC Santa Cruz</strong>, on the hill above
    town, where the road runs through dry grassland with Monterey Bay below. My wife had already been there on her
    own, with Wangcai, two weeks earlier; that visit is in <a href="/misc/campuses">the campuses post</a>. By
    evening we were in <strong>Carmel-by-the-Sea</strong>, forty-five miles down the coast, looking round a shop of
    soaps and candles before dinner at 6:39. Carmel is the storybook village on the
    Monterey Peninsula, all cottages and cypress trees. It famously has no street addresses (houses go by names, and
    residents pick up mail at the post office), and its best-known mayor was Clint Eastwood. It is also, by
    reputation, one of the most dog-friendly towns in America, which made it the right place to end this trip.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-car-window.webp'} alt="Wangcai sitting up on a car seat, ears up, looking out of the side window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the last morning, looking out of the window.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'carmel-dinner.webp'} alt="Plates of Chinese food on a table: stir-fried scallops with vegetables, noodles, and crispy fish with shredded vegetables." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last supper, in Carmel.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It was Chinese food: scallops stir-fried with peppers and carrots, a plate of noodles, crisp fried fish under a
    tangle of shredded vegetables, and soup. It was not glamorous, and it was exactly what you want after five days on
    the road. Then the trip ended the way trips do, with a long drive home down US-101 in the dark, and a plush
    banana slug, UC Santa Cruz's mascot, riding on the dashboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Lake, capitols, coast</h2>
  <p>
    Lined up in order, the photos make a strange little set. There are two state capitols a day apart, one a grand
    white dome with a mile-long mall, the other a sandstone courthouse with a silver hat. There is a river in
    Sacramento with a golden bridge and another in Reno full of ducks. There is a lake so clear you can see cloud
    shadows on the bottom, and an island with a tea house that has had no roof for decades. And at the end there is
    the Pacific, with one arch left standing.
  </p>
  <p>
    But mostly what the camera roll remembers is a dog. It remembers her on a ramp in Old Sacramento, at the bow of a
    pedal boat with her ears in the wind, watching ducks in Reno, as a bear at Sugar Pine Point, and wading into
    Lake Tahoe up to her shoulders. A trip with a dog is slower and more local than a trip without one. You spend more
    time at the water's edge and less time in museums. For five days at the end of a summer, that was exactly the
    point.
  </p>
